<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS entitlements, applied only when the bundle is code-signed at package
  time. `tauri dev` and unsigned/ad-hoc builds ignore this file entirely.

  keychain-access-groups is what the data-protection keychain requires before it
  will accept a biometry-protected item (secure_store::GateMode::Protected).
  Without it SecItemAdd returns errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018) and enrolment
  falls back to the verify-then-read gate.

  THIS ENTITLEMENT ONLY WORKS ALONGSIDE src-tauri/embedded.provisionprofile.
  It is provisioning-profile-gated: AMFI checks it against the profile embedded
  in the bundle, and if no profile grants it the kernel refuses to spawn the
  process — SIGKILL before any of our code runs, no crash report, and Finder
  showing only "The application "Rowel" can't be opened". Signing, notarization
  and Gatekeeper all still pass, which is why this shipped twice unnoticed
  (1.0.0-alpha.1 and 1.0.0-alpha.2). `bun scripts/check-macos-entitlements.mjs`
  now fails the release build rather than letting that recur.

  Verified on macOS 27 against the shipped alpha.2 binary, re-signed with the
  real Developer ID Application cert and hardened runtime, varying only the
  entitlements — note that the *value* made no difference, only the presence of
  the key:

    $(AppIdentifierPrefix)app.rowel.desktop  -> SIGKILL   (what alpha.2 shipped)
    UFBL3F444A.app.rowel.desktop             -> SIGKILL
    app.rowel.desktop  (no prefix at all)    -> SIGKILL
    + com.apple.application-identifier       -> SIGKILL
    no entitlements                          -> launches

  Never write Xcode's `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)` here. Tauri hands this file to
  `codesign` verbatim and expands nothing, so the variable ships as a literal
  group name. Use the team id, UFBL3F444A. The group must also be one the
  profile grants; a Developer ID profile typically grants `UFBL3F444A.*`.

  com.apple.security.application-groups puts the app in the App Group it shares
  with the Safari web extension (src-tauri/gen/safari): the browser host's
  socket is in that group's container, ~/Library/Group Containers/
  UFBL3F444A.app.rowel.desktop/browser.sock, the one place the sandboxed
  extension can connect to. The id is rowel_core::app::DESKTOP_APP_GROUP.

  Unlike keychain-access-groups this one is NOT profile-gated, and the profile
  does not grant it — but only because the id is macOS-style: the team id, a
  dot, a name. macOS grants such a group on the signature's team id alone
  (Apple DTS, developer forums thread 721701). An iOS-style `group.` id is
  checked against the provisioning profile on macOS 15 and later, and without
  a profile granting it the process is refused exactly as above. Never write a
  `group.` id here. check-macos-entitlements.mjs accepts a group under the
  team id without a profile and refuses any other.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
      <string>UFBL3F444A.app.rowel.desktop</string>
    </array>
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
      <string>UFBL3F444A.app.rowel.desktop</string>
    </array>
  </dict>
</plist>
